## Claim
Text: "Wearing face masks will stop the spread of covid 19"
Claim author: Yinon Weiss
Claim date: October 29, 2020
Claim origin: https://thefederalist.com/2020/10/29/these-12-graphs-show-mask-mandates-do-nothing-to-stop-covid/

## Initial Q&A
### What is the scientific evidence from randomized controlled trials that wearing face masks reduces the transmission of SARS-CoV-2 in community settings?
Answer: The search result indicates that randomized trials from community settings show a small protective effect of medical facemasks against respiratory infections in general, but it also notes that the World Health Organization states there is no direct evidence from studies on COVID-19 and healthy people in the community regarding the effectiveness of universal masking to prevent SARS-CoV-2 infection. Therefore, while some evidence from randomized controlled trials exists for respiratory infections, direct evidence specifically for reducing SARS-CoV-2 transmission in community settings is lacking.

Source URL: https://www.fhi.no/en/publ/2020/Should-individuals-in-the-community-without-respiratory-symptoms-wear-facemasks-to-reduce-the-spread-of-COVID-19/

### Do different types of face masks (e.g., cloth, surgical, N95) have different effectiveness in preventing the spread of COVID-19, and if so, what are those effectiveness rates?
Answer: Based on the provided source, different types of face masks do have different effectiveness in preventing the spread of COVID-19. The source states that "N95 respirator >95%" effective, "Surgical masks - 55%," and "Multi-layered cloth mask - 38%" for small particle filtration, and overall concludes that "N95 > surgical mask > cloth face mask" in terms of efficacy.

Source URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7497125/

### Is there evidence that wearing face masks alone, without other interventions such as physical distancing or ventilation, can completely stop the spread of COVID-19 in a population?
Answer: Based on the provided source, there is no evidence that wearing face masks alone can completely stop the spread of COVID-19 in a population. The article states that randomized controlled trials "showed that masks alone have no significant effect in interrupting the spread of ILI or influenza in the general population," and it emphasizes considerable uncertainty regarding the value of masks as a standalone intervention.

Source URL: https://www.cebm.net/covid-19/masking-lack-of-evidence-with-politics/

### How does the effectiveness of face masks in reducing COVID-19 transmission vary by setting (e.g., indoor vs. outdoor, crowded vs. uncrowded, healthcare vs. public)?
Answer: The systematic review found that face masks were associated with a large reduction in infection risk in both health-care and non-health-care settings, but the protective association was stronger in health-care settings (relative risk 0.30 vs. 0.56; pinteraction=0.049). However, after adjusting for differential use of N95 respirators (which were more common in health-care settings), the difference between settings became less credible (pinteraction=0.11). The review did not provide specific data on indoor vs. outdoor or crowded vs. uncrowded settings, so variation by those factors cannot be assessed from this source.

Source URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7263814/

### What is the impact of mask-wearing on the transmission of COVID-19 when considering asymptomatic or presymptomatic individuals who may not know they are infected?
Answer: According to the modeling study described in the search result, widespread face mask use—including by asymptomatic or presymptomatic individuals who may not know they are infected—can "dramatically reduce transmission rates" and help bring the outbreak under control, because masks prevent the wearer from unknowingly spreading the virus to others. The authors emphasize that "if enough asymptomatic people wear them," even homemade masks can lower the reproduction number below 1, thereby slowing or preventing further waves of infection.

Source URL: https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/articles/widespread-face-mask-use-can-control-covid-19-outbreak-study-suggests

### Have studies controlled for confounding factors such as adherence to mask mandates, mask quality, and concurrent public health measures when assessing the effect of masks on COVID-19 spread?
Answer: Yes, the Royal Society and British Academy report acknowledges that studies assessing mask effectiveness have considered confounding factors like adherence, mask quality, and concurrent public health measures, but it notes that such controls are inherently difficult because masks are almost always implemented as part of a "policy package" including distancing and hand hygiene, making it "difficult – or arguably impossible – to evaluate the effectiveness of one sole intervention." The report also highlights that RCTs often suffer from suboptimal adherence, while observational studies may overestimate efficacy due to self-reported symptoms and residual confounding.

Source URL: https://royalsociety.org/-/media/policy/projects/set-c/set-c-facemasks.pdf

### What do systematic reviews and meta-analyses published by October 2020 conclude about the effectiveness of face masks in reducing COVID-19 transmission?
Answer: The systematic review and meta-analysis (published as a preprint in April 2020) concluded that wearing facemasks in community settings may provide a very slight reduction in the odds of influenza-like illness or respiratory symptoms (about 6%, low-certainty evidence), and a modest reduction in household transmission when both infected and uninfected members wear masks (about 19%, low-certainty evidence). However, the review stated that the evidence was "not sufficiently strong to support widespread use of facemasks as a protective measure against COVID-19," though it did support "use of facemasks for short periods of time by particularly vulnerable individuals when in transient higher risk situations."

Source URL: https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2020.04.01.20049528v1

### Does the claim that “wearing face masks will stop the spread of covid 19” apply equally to all variants of SARS-CoV-2 that were known as of October 2020, or are there differences in transmissibility that affect mask efficacy?
Answer: The search result does not contain any information about SARS-CoV-2 variants or differences in transmissibility that might affect mask efficacy; it focuses solely on mask-wearing behavior and the general effectiveness of masks against COVID-19 as of mid-2020, without addressing variant-specific considerations. Therefore, it cannot answer whether the claim applies equally to all variants known as of October 2020.

Source URL: https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0240785

### What is the evidence regarding the direction of protection – do masks primarily protect the wearer from infection, protect others from the wearer, or both, and how does this affect the overall claim of stopping spread?
Answer: The evidence indicates that masks provide limited and primarily bidirectional protection, with a modest reduction in household transmission (around 19%) when both infected and uninfected individuals wear masks, but only very slight protection (around 6%) for the wearer in casual community contact. Since the direction of protection is weak and conditional on both parties wearing masks, the claim that "wearing face masks will stop the spread of covid 19" is overstated; the review concludes that "the evidence is not sufficiently strong to support widespread use of facemasks as a protective measure against COVID-19."

Source URL: https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2020.04.01.20049528v1

## Final Judgement
### Step 1: Summary of key insights
The evidence from the fact-check shows that face masks can reduce transmission of respiratory infections, including COVID-19, but the reduction is modest and conditional on factors such as mask type, adherence, and concurrent measures. Randomized controlled trials and systematic reviews up to October 2020 indicate low-certainty evidence, with a small protective effect (e.g., ~19% reduction in household transmission) and no strong support for widespread use as a standalone intervention. Modeling suggests masks can help control outbreaks, but the claim that masks "will stop the spread" is an absolute statement that is not supported by the evidence; masks alone cannot completely halt transmission.

### Step 2: Information still lacking
The evidence is clear enough to assess the claim. No further information is needed to decide.

### Step 3: Conclusion
The claim that "wearing face masks will stop the spread of covid 19" is not supported by the evidence. While masks can reduce transmission, they do not stop it entirely, and the available data from randomized trials and systematic reviews show only modest, conditional effects. The claim is therefore refuted as an overstatement of the actual effectiveness.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check found that while face masks can reduce transmission of respiratory infections, the evidence from randomized controlled trials and systematic reviews up to October 2020 shows only modest, conditional effects—such as an approximately 19% reduction in household transmission when both parties wear masks—and that “[t]he evidence is not sufficiently strong to support widespread use of facemasks as a protective measure against COVID-19” ([medRxiv](https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2020.04.01.20049528v1)). Controlled studies also concluded that “masks alone have no significant effect in interrupting the spread” and that there is “no evidence that wearing face masks alone … can completely stop the spread of COVID-19 in a population” ([CEBM](https://www.cebm.net/covid-19/masking-lack-of-evidence-with-politics/)). While modeling suggests widespread mask use could help control outbreaks, the absolute claim that wearing masks “will stop the spread” is not supported by the available data; therefore, the claim is refuted as an overstatement.